            <ArticleTitle>Pacinian Neuroma Presenting as Congenital Macrodactyly: A Rare Case Report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>PD22-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.7860/JCDR/2015/14525.6528</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Pacinian neuromas are rare benign tumours of pacinian corpuscles of the digits. They generally occur on the volar aspect of the fingertips. Pacinian corpuscles are specialised nerve endings with a lamellated structure and are regarded as pressure and vibration receptors. Here, we report a case of pacinian neuroma presenting as congenital macrodactyly of the right little finger with pain for which the lesion was surgically excised.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
